# Stable symlink for the FTDI FT232R that reaches the CyberPower PDU41002's
# serial console.
#
# Unlike 60-benchctrl-ch341.rules, this file grants **no** permissions and
# needs none: the kernel has an ftdi_sio driver, so the adapter appears as
# /dev/ttyUSB* owned root:dialout 0660 and the bench user is already in
# dialout. There is no libusb path here and no reason to open one.
#
# What this file is actually for is *identity*. The PDU is the one device on
# this bench that switches mains, and /dev/ttyUSB0 is a race: it is assigned in
# enumeration order, so plugging in a second USB-serial adapter can hand
# ttyUSB0 to something else while the driver still opens it and starts issuing
# oltctrl commands. Binding on the adapter's own serial number removes the
# ordering dependency.
#
# Scoped by serial number, not just VID/PID, and deliberately so: 0403:6001 is
# a generic FT232R that also appears in benchctrl's discovery GENERIC_BRIDGES
# list. A VID/PID-only symlink would point at whichever FTDI cable enumerated
# first. If the adapter is ever replaced, update ATTRS{serial} here — a
# symlink that silently stops appearing is a much better failure than one that
# silently points at a different cable.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0403", ATTRS{idProduct}=="6001", \
    ATTRS{serial}=="B0027PK6", SYMLINK+="benchctrl/pdu41002"

# Then open it by the symlink, never by ttyUSB0:
#
#   CyberPowerPDU41002.open(port="/dev/benchctrl/pdu41002",
#                           allowed_outlets=(1, 2))
#
# The network transport (open(host=...)) needs no udev rule at all — it is an
# outbound TCP connection to the PDU's SSH server, and the PDU's own hostname
# is the stable identifier.
